[ ] Move the saved queries to the application vocabulary =v5-queries $Rewrite every saved query in clearhead-cli src/queries and the CLI's sparql modules against the new graph, plus this workspace's .clearhead/queries/for-human.sparql. Row shapes stay as they are, so nvim and other clients do not change; the existing query tests are the net, so freeze their expected rows before rewriting. The competency queries in ontology v5/queries are the working templates. ADDED 2026-10-01: the graph will carry no storage facts (spec convention 3, the human's call: no file paths, lines, roots or calendar ids, since another backend such as sqlite would have none). Today's rows include source_file and source_line, used by nvim to jump to source, and some index queries join through the ws:Workspace node. Keep the rows unchanged by having the implementation add locations after the query, joining each row's IRI against the loaded workspace; remove every ws: pattern from the queries. REVISED 2026-10-01 with the human: rows lose source_file, source_line and charter_root instead of the CLI adding them back; clients get locations from the new locate command. The row shapes therefore do change for those columns, and nvim moves with them (nvim-locate). RETARGETED 2026-10-02 by Decision 45: queries use the application vocabulary (the spike's view queries are the template, about a third of v4's length); file and line stay in the graph, so rows keep their location columns and nvim is unchanged. Templates: docs/history/query-surface-spike/view/{for-human,agenda,unscheduled}.rq, which already return v4's answers on the spike workspace. ADDED 2026-10-04: v5-projection landed rdf::app::project_app beside v4 (clearhead-core 3e2ab63) so every commit stays green; this action also switches the CLI's dataset (query/dataset.rs project_dataset), JSON-LD output and export to it, passing the viewer's zone (Local) and app::Locations::of(&workspace). The ws: snapshot layer goes with it: app:file and app:line replace ws:hasSourceFile and ws:hasSourceLine.$ !2 ^2026-10-01T20:22 <01a0fb02-e644-7317-88ad-1e76ecfb2b0e #01a0faa2-71a3-70a1-91c5-a485dedd4de2
